["232 Chapter 8 In this chapter Why talk with a child about their epilepsy and seizures? . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 233 Talk with children of all ages . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 235 Communicating with babies (birth to 2 years) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .236 Talking with children 3 to 5 years old . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .238 How to talk about aura . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 240 Talking with children 6 to 8 years old . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 241 Prevent physical and sexual abuse of children with epilepsy . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .244 Talking about absence seizures . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 245 Promoting epilepsy awareness with puppets . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 247 Story: In Chile, puppets take the stage to shine a light on epilepsy . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 247 Talking with children 9 to 13 years old . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 249 How to stand up to bullies: Tips for young people . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 252 Talking with young people 14 to 18 years old . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 253 Sex and relationships . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 255 Talking with siblings about epilepsy . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .256 For health workers . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 259 Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","233 Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures Knowing about epilepsy and seizures helps children understand what is happening in their bodies. A child who knows they or someone else will be OK after a seizure is less likely to be scared or upset when they have one or see one. When children learn that medicine can prevent their seizures, it may make them feel more comfortable playing with their friends. And it may also help them remember to take their medicine every day. Family, friends, health workers, and others who encourage open and honest talking about epilepsy create an environment that better supports children who have seizures. At school, teachers can provide a child with support they may need and teach other students what to do if a seizure happens. Community education can highlight the benefits of accepting all children as they are, instead of focusing on what children with epilepsy cannot do and the problems they face. Children with epilepsy can learn that even though they may face challenges, there is nothing “wrong” with them. Adults can help children accept themselves by explaining why stigma and bullying happen and taking a clear stand against it. This will help children with epilepsy develop confidence to stand up for themselves. Why talk with a child about their epilepsy and seizures? Safety . Understanding their epilepsy can help a child prevent injury to themself and others. If a child learns how to tell a seizure is about to happen, they can stay safe by sitting down, alerting someone nearby, or calling for help. I don’t want my child’s epilepsy to hold her back. The more she knows about her epilepsy, what causes her seizures, and how to prevent them, the happier she’ll be. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 234 Less fear and worry . Children who know what to expect from their seizures and their recovery will feel less fear and worry and gain more confidence that they can handle their seizures. They will find reassurance and comfort when parents and others show concern and acceptance. Taking daily medicine . Children who understand even a little about how medicine can control their seizures become more aware of its importance and more willing to take it every day. Helping children understand what medicine does, even if it tastes bad or makes them sleepy, can make it easier for them to feel good about taking it. Reducing stigma . When people do not talk openly about epilepsy, fear of and discrimination against people with epilepsy can take root in the community and also inside the child. Adults talking with children about their epilepsy and children talking about their experiences with each other, caregivers, health workers, and community members, all contribute to fighting stigma. Parents and caregivers can support children to challenge stigma and misinformation by encouraging them to explore new experiences and enjoy life as fully as possible. For information about epilepsy stigma, see Chapter 5. Reducing feelings of guilt . A child may wonder if they caused their own epilepsy. Or if someone in the family has epilepsy, a child may think it is because of something they did. By explaining that epilepsy is a brain condition that many people have, children can learn that epilepsy is not anyone’s fault. Building peer support through shared experience . As a child better understands their experience of living with epilepsy, they are better able to connect with other children who also live with the condition. By sharing stories and experiences, children support each other, building confidence and community. What if I did something bad to make this happen? No, you did not. Epilepsy happens for many reasons, and sometimes no one knows exactly why. It’s like when it storms. The rain and thunder did not happen because of something you did. And you cannot control it. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","235Talk with children of all ages Benefits of talking to all children about epilepsy and seizures Because children thrive through playing, learning, and just being together, all children will benefit from understanding more about epilepsy. When a child has a seizure, children around them can prevent them from getting injured. When children understand what a seizure is, they will not feel afraid when they see someone having a seizure. The more children understand about epilepsy as a health condition, the less likely they are to bully or exclude a child who has seizures. Talk with children of all ages What a child will understand about epilepsy and seizures depends on their age. Parents and caregivers know and understand their children better than anyone else, including what words they may or may not understand, how they take in new information, and what questions they may ask. Your knowledge of and love for your children will help you talk with them about epilepsy. No matter their age, talking with a child about living with epilepsy does not mean you must have all the answers or share everything you know. Young children who are just learning to use words need small bits of information rather than everything all at once. As children grow and change, they will want to know more, including why they have seizures. It is OK to say you do not know. Together you will learn how to talk openly and honestly about epilepsy, and together you will learn how to best manage their condition. She is already asking you a lot of questions, right? I told my son who is the same age that sometimes our bodies do things we can’t control, like hiccup or burp. When his body moves suddenly, it is something like that, and it’s called a seizure. How can I explain to my daughter that she has epilepsy? She’s only 4! Yes. She asks why her body sometimes moves by itself. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 236 Communicating with babies (birth to 2 years) Seizures happen more often in babies and infants than at any other time in childhood. They can be caused by epilepsy or by fevers, difficult births, or other illness. Seizures in a baby can be sudden and short. Seizures with convulsions or other body movements can be hard to tell apart from other ways a baby moves their body, like flapping their arms, stretching their entire body, and eye movements. By watching a baby’s movements, parents and caregivers can get clues about possible seizures, so the baby gets the right treatment. For example, repetitive movements like chewing, smacking lips, and leg-bicycling that happen many times close together is a possible seizure. The same actions happening just once or occasionally are not likely to be seizures. Helping seizure recovery . We cannot know what a baby feels when they come out of a seizure, but they may feel anxious, confused, or uncomfortable. When a seizure ends and awareness returns, reassure them by holding them, speaking with them, and acknowledging that something happened and you are still there. Stay connected by maintaining eye contact or physical touch. It can be helpful to stay quiet and keep the lights dim. A calm environment that is easy on the senses may make it easier for a baby to recover from a seizure and feel safe. Communicating without words . Because they cannot speak, babies communicate with sounds and movements. For example, a baby cries, frowns, or squirms when wet or hungry. Cooing noises can mean your baby is feeling happy and comfortable. As you learn what sounds and facial expressions your baby makes at different times, you will begin to notice when there is something new or different in their mood and behavior. Giving babies medicine . Taking daily antiseizure medicine may make a baby irritable or uncomfortable. A baby may try to avoid taking medicine by turning their head away from it, coughing, or spitting it out. Distract or calm your baby with love, care, and attention by speaking softly, singing, or gently massaging them when you give medicine. Make up a simple, reassuring song that lets the child know it is medicine time. Camila’s back! You are fine, Camila, and your mama is here with you. Time to mix the medicine that Mai likes to take... Put it in the bottle and shake, shake, shake... Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","237Communicating with babies (birth to 2 years) Seizure first aid training. If you need to leave a baby who has seizures with an older child or another caregiver, make sure the person knows what to do when a seizure happens. For information about seizure first aid, see Chapter 2. Caring for fever seizures . A high fever (above 38°C or 100.4°F) may cause a seizure in a newborn or infant. When the fever lowers, the seizures typically go away but may return if the baby has another high fever. For information about fever seizures, see Chapter 3, page 59. For information about medicines to reduce fever, see Chapter 4, page 114. Finding support . Caring for an infant and toddler, whether they have epilepsy or not, can be tiring. Ask for help, take time to rest, and be patient with yourself and your child. Your child’s epilepsy should not stop you from reaching out for support from family and friends, or from finding ways to enjoy the time you share with your baby. For the health worker . When you care for a baby who has epilepsy or seizures, communicate as clearly as you can with parents and other caregivers. What causes seizures and how to care for a baby with epilepsy or seizures are complex topics. As you discuss these issues, share the most up-to-date medical information you have. When you cannot answer a question confidently, you can say, “I will find out and let you know”—and be sure to follow up. Make sure parents know how to reach you for advice or during an emergency. It can also be helpful to arrange support from other parents of children with epilepsy. Identify or start support groups in your community to help connect people. For information about support groups, see Chapter 5, page 136. Communicate with children who have rare epilepsy syndromes Parents and caregivers of children with rare childhood epilepsy syndromes, such as Dravet syndrome, Lennox-Gastaut syndrome (LGS), and West syndrome, among others, need additional support. These usually require lifetime care and management. Children with these syndromes may have delays learning to speak, difficulty speaking, or may remain nonverbal, but not all children will have the same level of disability. You can support their attempts to communicate with gestures, facial expressions, and signs. Guidance from a speech pathologist may help. Teaching them (and you!) the sign language used in your country will help their mental and social development. For information about rare childhood epilepsy syndromes, see Chapter 3, page 60. HOW TO Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 238 Talking with children 3 to 5 years old Children usually begin to notice and question things happening in their bodies after their third or fourth birthdays. They often know when they are sick and can understand explanations about what is happening and why. Listen closely to a child’s questions and respond simply and clearly. Although a child may not understand words like “epilepsy” and “seizures,” parents and caregivers can begin using these terms so they become familiar. Why seizures happen . Explain to the child that seizures happen to many people because of different health problems, and sometimes nobody knows why they happen. Let them know seizures are not something to be afraid of and that having seizures does not have to stop them from doing what they love. Children with seizures can still do things they enjoy, like playing, drawing, and learning. Encourage the child to ask you any questions they may have or tell you if they are worried about something. Remind them that you love them and that their seizures are just one way they are special. Asking for help . By age 4, children can understand that they might get hurt if they have convulsions that make them fall. It is a good time to share simple information about seizure first aid. Explain basic ideas, like listening to their body for warning signs, and sitting down or telling someone if they feel they are about to have a seizure (see “How to talk about aura,” page 240). Be sure they understand that there is nothing shameful about having a seizure, and if they need any kind of help afterwards, they should ask an older child or adult. When your body moves suddenly all by itself, that is called a seizure. It happens when your brain gets mixed up for a moment. Then it ends. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","239Talking with children 3 to 5 years old What happens during their seizures . Drawings and pictures can get a child’s attention and help them understand the brain and seizures. Share a picture or make a drawing that gives an idea of what is happening in the child’s brain during a seizure. Helping your child’s community understand seizures . Just as you teach your child about epilepsy and seizures, you can also talk with their siblings, cousins, and other playmates. Children understand at a young age that people’s bodies don’t all look or act the same way and differences do not stop them from playing together. With support from adults or older children, young children are good at learning how to care for each other. Introducing basic steps of seizure first aid can be both helpful and fun (see Chapter 2). Families of a child’s friends and neighbors may not have experience with epilepsy and may have wrong or strange ideas about it. Help them learn the basics about epilepsy so they do not pass misinformation to others. All parents and caregivers can teach children to speak up and give correct information when they hear a person say something about epilepsy that is not true. This is a brain and these are the electrical signals happening all the time inside it. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 240 Talk about aura An aura is a sign or physical sensation that is considered the beginning of a seizure, but may be felt minutes before a seizure happens. A child who can feel, understand, and tell others about an aura can often avoid getting hurt during a seizure. Parents, teachers, caregivers, and community members can provide better support when a child recognizes an aura and speaks up. Not everyone who has seizures will feel an aura, but it is helpful if they do. People feel auras differently. One person may get a certain taste in their mouth, someone else may feel “butterflies” in their stomach, and another person may see unusual colors. Many children enjoy drawing. Invite a child to draw the unusual feelings their aura causes. HOW TO No, the warning sign is like a friend helping you to stay safe because a seizure is happening. What do you notice right as your seizures start? Does your tummy ache? Or do you feel dizzy? Sometimes I feel a little dizzy. That could be a warning sign you are going to have a seizure. When that happens, stop what you are doing and find a safe place to sit. Tell the person with you, “I feel a seizure coming.” Does it mean something is wrong? Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","241Talking with children 6 to 8 years old Talking with children 6 to 8 years old As children get older, they are able to understand more about their bodies in general as well as more about their brain, epilepsy, and seizures. Parents and caregivers can expand on the simple information they shared earlier. Children at these ages are better able to take their medicine properly, avoid things that trigger their seizures, protect themselves from injury, and help when they see someone having a seizure. What happens during their seizures . Children at this age need to know what happens so they can protect themselves, know what they can ask of others, and what to do after they have a seizure. Make sure they know if they may fall or drop things they are holding so they and others around them can try to make the seizure as safe as possible. A child is likely to feel embarrassed if their seizures cause them to wet or soil themself. Help the child understand this is part of their seizure, not because they are “babies.” Speak openly with your child’s teachers, school nurse, or administrator about how some people lose bladder or bowel control during a seizure. Ask them to avoid drawing attention to your child if it happens to them. Pack an extra set of clothes in your child’s backpack or leave clothes with a teacher so they can help your child change after a seizure. If the child will need to sleep or rest quietly after a seizure, make sure they and their teacher know it is OK to need this. Encourage the child to explain to their friends that resting helps them recover. Avoiding seizure triggers . If you know what triggers a child’s seizures, talk together about how to avoid those triggers. For example, depending on the trigger, you might suggest that the child not eat too much sugary food, not exercise so much they get out of breath, avoid too much time in the sun, stop playing when they feel tired, or rest more when they have a cold or infection. Why seizures happen . Find a way to explain that your child will understand. Here are some examples that might work for you and your child: Our brains send electrical signals that tell our bodies what to do. It’s like watching television, when sometimes the picture and sound suddenly stop. When the signals start flowing properly again, everything goes back to normal. Something like that happens during a seizure. Electrical signals in the brain get mixed up and make the body shake and fall. Then, after a minute, it wakes up again. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 242 Taking medicine . Children at this age tend to like and depend on routines. Parents and caregivers still have to remind and support them, but can encourage children to take responsibility for remembering to take their antiseizure medicine. It is like a mobile phone. Sometimes, you are talking to someone and the call drops. Then they call again and you continue talking. That’s sort of what happens with your body and brain during a seizure—it drops off, and it comes back. It’s like heavy traffic, but instead of too many cars, it’s too many messages in your brain all at once. Everything stops, and then it starts going again. Make a chart: Medicine superstars Draw a simple chart with rows and columns for each day of the week (see example below). Explain to your child that every time they take their medicine on time, they get to mark the chart with a star or other shape. When your child earns 7 marks in a row, offer a small reward such as an extra story or a weekend activity, anything that works for your family. ACTIVITY Ira’s medicine chart Sun SatMon Tue Wed FriThu Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","243Talking with children 6 to 8 years old Asking for help . Having a seizure is nothing to be ashamed of. If the child knows they are going to have a seizure, they can ask for help to stay as safe as possible. If they are feeling poorly while recovering from a seizure, they can also ask for help to get what they need to feel better—a drink of water, dry clothing, a quiet place to rest. The more people who know about a child’s epilepsy and what they need to recover, the easier it will be for them to avoid harm and regain comfort. Fighting stigma and wrong ideas . If a child tells you that someone has treated them poorly because of their epilepsy, offer words of comfort and remind them that not everyone understands epilepsy. Explain that people are often afraid of things they do not understand and will make things up or repeat things they have heard others say, even if they are not true. Talk with the child about how they can speak up against teasing and bullying, and let them know you will support them. Help them think about and practice what they could say to a person who insults them (see “How to stand up to bullies: Tips for young people,” page 252). Ask your child if they would like you to talk about epilepsy with other parents or reach out to teachers or a neighborhood group to organize a seizure first aid training. Be sure to follow through on what you offer to do. Invite them to help if they want to. Speaking out against stigma is not the responsibility of a child with epilepsy or their family alone. Children learn by watching adults around them, and parents and caregivers need to prevent bullying and instead act to create communities where kindness and respect are the norm. Accept differences among people, pay attention when others speak, oppose bullying, and praise your child when they share toys or help another person to model behaviors you would like them to develop. Sasha called me “crazy girl” and started waving her arms to make fun of me! It made me feel like I never want to play with her again! Oh, Tuki, I am so sorry. You know what Sasha is saying is not true. Some children wear glasses, some use a wheelchair, and you have something different in your brain. That is part of what makes you you, and everybody loves you! Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 244 Prevent physical and sexual abuse of children with epilepsy Children with epilepsy are at a higher risk of being physically and sexually abused than other children. Because they are often seen as weak or different, others may try to control, frighten, or exploit them. When they lose consciousness during a seizure, or need time to recover from a seizure, they become more vulnerable to abuse. Usually, abuse happens in secret and is carried out by someone the person knows. Talk to children about their right to keep their bodies private and say no. Be aware of signs of physical and sexual abuse: • unexplained bruises or strange marks on skin • fear of certain people • sudden changes in behavior • a child becoming quieter or withdrawn Pay attention when a child shows fear or asks for help. If you suspect a child is being harmed, talk with a health worker or another trusted adult about what to do. By openly addressing everyone’s right to be free of physical and sexual abuse, all children, including those with epilepsy, are more likely to be protected. As you strengthen a child’s self-respect and self-confidence, they learn that they will be heard and supported when they speak about their feelings and experiences. Parents, caregivers, and community leaders can work together to create safe environments at home, school, and in the community, and to hold abusers accountable. For information about child abuse and how to help, see Hesperian’s Promoting Community Mental Health, Chapter 5 . Your body belongs to you. It’s OK to say “no” if someone tries to touch you in a way that feels uncomfortable. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","245Talking with children 6 to 8 years old Talking about absence seizures While absence seizures can happen at any age, they are most commonly recognized when children are between 6 and 8 years old. What happens during their seizures . Children who have absence seizures may suddenly stop moving, stare blankly, or smack their lips. These episodes usually last only 3 to 15 seconds, but they may happen many times during a day—sometimes even hundreds of times! Usually, a child having an absence seizure will not know it is happening. Helping a child with absence seizures . First, explain to the child that their absence seizures are a health condition and not something they can control. When you notice an absence seizure happening, guide the child away from any danger and stay with them until they are alert again. Then begin to organize a safety net and support system by telling people close to the child—siblings, neighbors, the child’s friends and their families—what an absence seizure is and what to do when one happens. No, it is neither good nor bad, it just is. And we let you know so you don’t miss things. Is that bad? Sometimes your brain takes a very short nap during the day. It happens a lot, and you just space out for a few seconds and don’t notice what’s going on around you. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 246 What you see during an absence seizure . If you are looking at a child having an absence seizure, you might mistakenly think they are not paying attention. It may take a while before you recognize that a child’s blank stare could be a type of seizure. Absence seizures at school . The brief, painless episodes of “zoning out” during absence seizures are typically followed by a rapid recovery. It is important for parents to talk to teachers so they understand it is not a problem of “not paying attention.” Parents can encourage children to feel comfortable talking to teachers about what they experience. When teachers listen without being judgemental and offer to help rather than dismiss what the child has to say, they help to build trust and safety in the school. Sir, somehow, I didn’t know about the assignment. I feel like I miss many things… Everything goes quiet in my brain for a few seconds. It’s like I’m listening to you in class, but I don’t always hear what you say. That is worrisome, Maria. But I’m glad you told me about this so we can look into what might help. If you notice Gladys staring blankly or “zoning out” for a few seconds, don’t be alarmed or raise your voice. Gladys gets absence seizures that make her lose focus and attention for a few seconds. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","247Talking with children 6 to 8 years old In Chile, puppets take the stage to shine a light on epilepsy Community health promoters in Chile created and filmed a puppet show to raise awareness about epilepsy in elementary and middle schools. The video features puppet Juanito, who attends school with his friends and has a caring and supportive family. One day during recess, Juanito has a seizure on the school playground. His alarmed but quick-thinking classmates rush to tell their teacher that Juanito needs help. After making sure Juanito is safe, the teacher asks the students to describe what they saw. This not only helped the teacher understand the situation but also educated the children about seizure symptoms. After Juanito recovers, the teacher speaks with him gently and calmly. She suspects Juanito may have epilepsy. We follow Juanito as he visits the doctor with his parents, spends time at home with his grandmother, and plays with friends at the playground. In each setting, Juanito’s experiences spark conversations about epilepsy, spreading awareness among different community members. To see if the video helped children learn, the community health promoters asked students some questions about epilepsy before and after showing it. Even children who had not heard of epilepsy before knew what to do after watching it when asked, “What should you do if your friend has a seizure?” Promoting epilepsy awareness with puppets Puppets are a great way to teach children about epilepsy. Children (and adults!) are attracted by the entertainment of a puppet show. A good story and a few memorable characters make it much easier to communicate about and understand difficult or sensitive topics. Children with epilepsy who might not be able to talk about their feelings can learn from puppets that mirror their feelings, teaching them and others what it can feel like to have epilepsy. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 248 Make puppets at home Simple puppets to entertain and teach children about epilepsy can be made at home. Puppet stories can describe familiar experiences, the adventures of epilepsy superheroes, and everything in between. Puppets can be made from paper glued onto sticks, using simple materials like something to draw with (markers, crayons, colored pencils), paper or cloth scraps, scissors, short sticks, and glue (or needle and thread). Or you can make sock puppets. Together with your child, draw a few faces on paper or make them out of scraps of cloth. Glue or sew each face onto a stick or sock. Let the child name each puppet. Use them to tell stories about different topics, like what happens when a person has a seizure, what questions a doctor might ask about seizures, or how to help when someone is having a seizure. A puppet made from a sock looks alive! 1. Fit the sock over your hand. 2. Make the mouth by pushing in the cloth between your thumb and fingers. 3. Add eyes, nose, and hair to the sock. HOW TO Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","249Talking with children 9 to 13 years old Talking with children 9 to 13 years old Children these ages are developing more independence and are learning more from life outside the home—in school, with friends, from sports and other organized and informal groups, and from all forms of media. They may express themselves in new ways and look more to peers for support. The physical changes of puberty also affect how children think and feel. Changes in height, weight, and hormones can affect their medication needs. If a child’s seizures suddenly return after having been controlled, they may need a dose adjustment or a different medicine. Talk with children about how body changes might affect their epilepsy. As a child with epilepsy grows more independent, talk with them about how their expanding social circle means they will meet people who do not know about their epilepsy and may have wrong ideas about it. Encourage them to wear an epilepsy bracelet (see Chapter 6, page 164) and help them practice explaining what it means to have epilepsy and seizures, so they feel ready when the topic comes up. Being safe doing physical activities . Parents and caregivers may worry about and discourage or stop the participation of a child with epilepsy in sports and other physical activities. Instead of placing limits on your child, continue to treat epilepsy as just one part of their life that does not have to stop them from doing things they want to do. Talk with your child about what they will do if a seizure happens during different activities. How can they prepare for a “breakthrough seizure” if one happens because their medicine stops working as well? What can they do if they feel aura? Will your child have a “buddy” with them or is there someone else who knows how to help them in case of a seizure? Do they need safety equipment, like a helmet? Talk with your child about how to avoid things they know might trigger their seizures, such as thirst (carry a water bottle), hunger (bring a snack), etc. Help your child strengthen their self-confidence and creativity by problem-solving together . I know you love swimming. What can we do to make sure you’re safe if a seizure happens in the water? Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 250 Sometimes children may feel safety measures, such as wearing a helmet while bicycling or never swimming alone, draw too much attention to them or their epilepsy, or make the activities less fun. But often, these safety measures are good ways to protect everyone, not just people with epilepsy. Talk about safety during physical activities with everyone not just with your child. Taking medicine . Children at this age can take on more responsibility for themselves, including taking their daily antiseizure medicine on their own. It is normal for people of any age to forget sometimes. Try to find a way to help your child remember without causing tension or nagging. Offer to help . Even as older children become more independent, they may sometimes feel they are not completely ready to handle the increased responsibility of being “grown up.” They might not want to admit feeling insecure, seeing it as a sign of weakness or immaturity. When you notice their responsibilities seem to be causing them too much stress, try to lessen their burden before they ask. Of course I can do it myself. I’m not a baby! ...But what if I forget? Diego, I know you get tired of me interrupting to give you your daily medicine. What do you think about taking it on your own? Hmm... why don’t we tape the medicine chart to the wall, and you mark each dose as you take it? Then I won’t bother you unless I see it wasn’t marked. OK. But you’ll have to tie a pen on a string to it too. I can never find a pen when I need one. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","251Talking with children 9 to 13 years old Navigating social relationships . As children approach their teenage years, fitting in with friends usually becomes more important to them. For children with epilepsy, these relationships can be especially challenging. A child may feel self-conscious about having seizures or needing to be extra careful. They may resent being treated like “the kid who needs special attention because of epilepsy.” Parents and caregivers often face the tricky challenge of supporting a child without getting in the way. At these ages, your child may want to handle their friendships and other social situations and relationships on their own. You can help by being a guide rather than taking over or making your child feel pressured into doing what you suggest. For instance, be sure to talk with your child about any plans or ideas you have for community education about epilepsy, so they know what to expect. Listen to how they feel about this and ask if they might like to be involved, but do not pressure them if they prefer not to participate. Bullying . Children and youth with epilepsy too often experience bullying and teasing from other children and adults. This can cause a child to withdraw from learning and social activities or drop out of school. Bullying creates stress, which is a common trigger for seizures. The more people know about epilepsy and its causes, the less likely they are to engage in or tolerate bullying and ridicule. Everyone—parents, teachers, neighbors, and community members—has a role to play in handling bullies and creating an environment in which bullying is not OK. When adults take the lead, children can feel more confident standing up against bullying. My friend Eliza is going to talk to your school principal about holding a seizure first aid training. I think it would be good for other people to be the ones to bring up epilepsy, not me. What do you think? Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 252 Stand up to bullies: Tips for young people No one has to accept bullying and teasing. Here are some ways to respond if you are bullied because you have epilepsy: Be confident in yourself and remember that epilepsy does not define you. Focus on your strengths—your talents, the activities you enjoy and are good at, and the goals you are working toward. Remember all the reasons why your friends and family love you. Let this awareness help you stay calm. Practice not showing you are upset. Bullies often seek a reaction. Not reacting can make them lose interest. Confront a bully by being assertive. Tell the bully to stop what they are doing. For example, use your strong voice to say: “Stop teasing me about my condition. It is not OK. You are wrong and mean to treat me this way!” Stay connected with supportive friends who appreciate you and will stand up for you. Talk with an adult you trust who is good at listening. Share your experience and feelings with a parent, teacher, sports coach, or neighbor instead of keeping them to yourself. Practice what you might say if you are bullied again. Report bullying to a person with authority at your school, community council, or church. Ask them to inform you of what action they plan to take to address what happened. Bring a friend with you so you feel supported and also have a witness to how they respond. As long as you are not being physically attacked, standing up to bullies does not mean physically fighting back. It means speaking up confidently. If you think you are in danger, seek help from adults that you trust. HOW TO That would be so great, Mama. It will really help. I’m so sorry to hear about the teasing that has been going on, but I’m glad you told me. It’s not OK. Let’s practice what you can say to your principal about it. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","253Talking with young people 14 to 18 years old Talking with young people 14 to 18 years old Happiness and safety for young people with epilepsy increasingly extends beyond the daily activities of home and school life. As they grow older, teens take on more responsibility—they set their own goals, solve problems themselves, and learn what they like and don’t like. Still, they need adult support. Help teens feel heard and supported . Give your teen space. This can mean letting them know you are there for them, but the decision to talk is up to them. While it is important to show interest in their life outside the home, asking too many questions can feel invasive and push them away. Teens often share less with parents than they did when they were younger. When they do open up, be prepared for lots of emotions, including anger, frustration, and sadness. Show the teen you are comfortable listening to them express their feelings and try to listen without judging, even when their views are different than yours. This helps build and maintain trust between you. Peer pressure . Young people often feel they have to go along with what others their age are saying and doing. Peer pressure is social pressure to act as others do, in order to be liked, included, and respected. It can lead to behaviors that may be dangerous, such as using drugs or alcohol, because a person fears saying “no” will make their friends dislike them, reject them, or see them as unworthy of friendship. This is as true for teens with epilepsy as it is for other youth. Talking with teens about alcohol and drugs is important. When a young person’s brain is still developing, use of alcohol and drugs can be harmful. For someone with epilepsy, alcohol and drugs may trigger seizures or interact badly with antiseizure medicines, causing unwanted side effects and changing how well seizures are controlled. Using drugs or alcohol can also cause other problems, such as missing a medicine dose while “sleeping off” a hangover. Peer pressure to physically look a certain way can also be challenging for teens with epilepsy. Some teens may feel pressured to lose weight, so they skip meals as a form of “dieting.” This is particularly unhealthy if hunger is a seizure trigger. Others may feel pressure to build up their muscles, so they start a new or more intense exercise program. This may make the tiredness caused by some antiseizure medications even harder to manage. I know it can be hard, but if you could talk about how alcohol mixes badly with your epilepsy medicine, your friends may respect your need not to drink. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 254 Managing screen time . Young people with epilepsy are just as likely as anyone else to spend a lot of time on mobile or computer screens. The use of electronic devices can bring both positives (like learning and connecting with friends) and negatives (like lost sleep, stress, unsafe interactions, or even seizures, if their use is a trigger). Encourage your teen to talk with you about their experiences online and how they can navigate the digital world. If the teen is worried about telling friends they cannot spend time online, offer to practice having conversations about setting limits and finding alternatives. For information about ways to stay safe and protect your privacy online, see page 144. Getting enough sleep . Because bodies and minds change so much during these years, young people also need more sleep. This can be difficult as the amount of schoolwork increases and teens want to enjoy time with friends later into the evening. Not getting enough sleep or enough good quality sleep can trigger seizures. Encourage 8 to 10 hours of nighttime sleep every day. For more information on sleeping well with epilepsy, see page Chapter 6, page 192. Fighting stigma and wrong ideas . When your teen is feeling low about having epilepsy, supporting their connections with other young people can be especially helpful. Encourage teens to seek out peer groups or online communities where youth can share experiences, build friendships, and support one another with their challenges. Getting involved in community activities or volunteering to raise epilepsy awareness can help them feel included and valued as they develop new skills and confidence. For more on stopping epilepsy stigma, see Chapter 5. Planning for adulthood . Teens with or without epilepsy begin to think about their future and the kind of life they want as an adult. Epilepsy does not mean they cannot work, study, or have a family, but they may need extra support or face job discrimination due to their condition. Encourage the teen to talk about their interests and dreams, and to identify people they can rely on for guidance and friendship. Remind them that epilepsy is only one part of who they are and will not stop them from building a meaningful life. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","255Sex and relationships Sex and relationships Having epilepsy does not affect a person’s ability to have feelings for other people or the desire to get closer to someone. Neither does epilepsy change the body’s ability to experience and enjoy romantic or sexual feelings. Like everyone else, a person with epilepsy can decide if, when, and how they want to, or do not want to, explore sex or a romantic relationship. For teens and young people Telling someone you are interested in or already dating about your epilepsy does not mean you are sharing a weakness. It is just sharing part of who you are. It is natural to feel nervous about having these conversations, but talking openly can help build trust and understanding. As someone with epilepsy, you already have experience paying attention to and talking about your body and feelings. This may help you feel comfortable talking about relationships and sex with a potential sexual partner. You can decide what to share and when. When you are ready, share basic information about: • epilepsy as a brain condition… it is not contagious! • what happens when you have a seizure. • any seizure triggers you may have. • seizure first aid. You have probably already experienced how epilepsy can affect relationships—sometimes it strengthens bonds between people, other times it frightens people away. When you open up about your epilepsy, you can expect questions and comments that will tell you something about what the person knows or thinks about epilepsy. How they respond to you may also help you decide to continue seeing this person or notice “red flags.” If a person rejects you because of epilepsy or seizures, it means you would not have been able to trust a relationship with them anyway. I wonder if you’ll still like me when I tell you about my epilepsy? I wonder if I am ready to find out? Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 256 Safe sex . Sex can be as healthy and enjoyable for people with epilepsy as it can be for people who do not have epilepsy. However, some antiseizure medicines make some kinds of birth control less effective. Other medicines may lower desire, as can fear of having a seizure while being sexual. If you’re feeling unsure about whether to have sex with someone, find an adult who understands these issues and is willing to discuss your decisions with you, perhaps a relative or someone in the community you trust who also has epilepsy. For information about how to protect yourself from unintended pregnancy or how to plan for a pregnancy with epilepsy, see Chapter 7. For parents: Talking with your teen about sex and relationships It is common to feel uncomfortable talking with your child about sex and relationships. But developing an interest in sex and romantic relationships is often a part of growing up, and knowing how to do that safely is important for everyone. If you find it difficult to discuss sex and relationships with your teen, you can encourage them to reach out to someone with whom they feel safe—a relative, family friend, teacher, counselor, health worker, or an adult who also has epilepsy. If your teen cannot find someone they are comfortable speaking with about sexuality and dating, ask a health worker to recommend a counselor or other adult who is skilled at talking with and supporting young people. Talking with siblings about epilepsy When a child has epilepsy, everyone in the family is affected, including the child’s siblings. Because adult family members may need to take extra time to care for the needs of the child with epilepsy, the other children may resent that their brother or sister is getting more attention than they are. Or they may feel pressure to be “good” and take on extra responsibilities to make up for the challenges that epilepsy is causing their parents. Especially with young children, this can lead to “acting out” and conflicts in the family. Talking openly about epilepsy with the entire family helps everyone develop a common understanding of what is happening and why it is important to get a child’s seizures under control. It also gives other children a chance to talk about how they feel and express feelings of anger, fear, or sadness, as well as concern for one another. Parents and caregivers can share how much they love and appreciate everyone in the family. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","257Talking with siblings about epilepsy I’m sorry. Your aunt Grace was supposed to help, but now she can’t. Especially with the added stress that comes with an epilepsy diagnosis, it can be difficult for parents and caregivers to remain patient and caring with each child all the time. If possible, try to make regular times to do “special” activities with each child. They will appreciate the attention, and you can learn how epilepsy is affecting them and their needs. Older siblings are sometimes expected to “watch out for” and care for younger children or a child with epilepsy. While this often happens naturally given the love among children in a family, it can also create tension. As siblings get older and want to spend more time with friends their own age, they may not always want to include a sibling, whether or not the child has epilepsy. Acknowledge how much you value and appreciate the care siblings give the child with epilepsy. Help siblings understand how fear of epilepsy and social stigma make it harder for a child with epilepsy to spend time with playmates and friends their age. Also try to arrange for other caregivers so the older siblings have some days each week for social time free from babysitting. Not again! I don’t get any time just with my friends. What if we decide to go someplace else? James, I have to ask you to bring Ode with you to the playground. I have to work and there is no one else who can make sure he is OK. I’ll be here to look after Ode all weekend, so you won’t have to. Oh, all right. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 258 No one wants to see a child with epilepsy get hurt, either physically or emotionally. Like parents, the child’s siblings may become so focused on protecting a child with epilepsy that they miss how the child’s abilities increase with age. Protection is important for children, but being too protective can limit a child’s growth and confidence. While keeping safety in mind, siblings can encourage a child with epilepsy to try new things like other children their age. Why don’t you kids go and play? But that is not fair! Misha deserves to be included. She is my sister, and I love her. I don’t want to. I had a seizure on the court last time, remember? Everyone called me names. I remember. But this time I will be with you. Who cares what anyone thinks. You’re awesome! Hey Misha, let’s go play badminton. It will be fun! What if my friends think Misha is weird? I don’t want them making fun of her or her epilepsy. Maybe it’s better if we stay here. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","259For health workers For health workers Children with epilepsy can benefit greatly when health workers and adults outside the family develop relationships with them. Simple actions like making eye contact, waving hello, or stopping to talk even briefly can help them feel seen and know they are valued. While wearing a white medical jacket might command respect from your coworkers, it may frighten children who only see people wearing them when something scary has happened with their health. You can help children with epilepsy feel more comfortable by wearing more colorful, casual clothes—which are also often easier to keep clean. Showing a personal interest in the child in front of you helps builds trust. Starting health visits with questions like, “What grade are you in?” or “What games do you like to play with your friends?” can help a child feel comfortable talking with you for the first time, or as someone they have met before but not seen recently. Even though you may be pressed for time, it is worth building a relationship with your young patients. Sometimes a child’s answers will clue you in to how they are feeling about themself or what they are concerned about. By allowing a conversation to flow naturally, you may learn helpful information that a routine set of questions might not reveal. For older children, you might also ask, “How do your teachers and classmates feel about your epilepsy?” or “Do you think epilepsy stops you from doing activities you enjoy or would like to try?” When explaining things to children, think of ways to be as clear as possible. Because everyone communicates and understands things differently, you can increase understanding when you: Use easy words, plain language, and speak slowly . Avoid medical words or complicated terms. Unfortunately, the language of epilepsy and seizures is filled with them! For example, instead of “seizure management,” say “what to do when a seizure happens.” Using common words helps a child pay attention to your message, rather than confusing them with information they must try too hard to understand. When I talk with a child, I want them to understand I care about them. I start by asking, “What do you like to do at school?” instead of asking about epilepsy or seizures. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026","Talking with children about epilepsy and seizures 260 Repeat key points and ask a child to repeat them back to you . After explaining something important, repeat it in the same simple way. Then ask the child to say it back to you in their own words. For example, when talking about aura, you can say, “If you feel dizzy, sit down right away. Can you tell me what you will do if you feel dizzy?” Divide complex instructions into small steps . For example, instead of saying, “Take 1 pill with water, 10 minutes after a meal, every day,” you can say, “One, eat breakfast. Two, wait 10 minutes. Three, pop one tablet out of this medicine strip and swallow the tablet with plenty of water. Now repeat that all back to me and count out each step using your fingers.” When it comes to learning about a child’s health, you may need to rely on information from parents or other caregivers. But it is important to hear directly from children about how their seizures and medicines make them feel. With older children and teens, always include them in conversations about their health so they do not feel left out, undervalued, or invisible. I want teens to understand I care about them and value their ideas. I start by asking to speak with them alone, without their parents. Then, if questions come up that they can’t answer, we invite the adults in. Epilepsy and Seizures: Building Care in the Community 2026"]
